IMPORT daydate

-- Public Interest Disclosure Act 2010 (Qld) -- the date arithmetic this baseline needs, kept in one small module so
-- that the daydate library is imported once. Every other module of this encoding imports this one, not daydate.
--
-- The Act states two periods a discloser meets: "within 6 months after the date the disclosure was made" (s 20(1)(b)
-- (iii)) and "within 28 days after receiving the written reasons" (s 30(3)). The Act itself says nothing about how a
-- period is reckoned. That is the Acts Interpretation Act 1954 (Qld), which is not deposited in Commons, so its rules are
-- not encoded here: the day of the event is excluded, the period ends on its last day, and a last day on a weekend or a
-- public holiday is NOT moved (NOTES.md, fork F15 and finding 9).

§ `Public Interest Disclosure Act 2010 (Qld) -- dates`

-- A date written day, month, year (for the cases file, which does not import daydate).
GIVEN d IS A NUMBER, m IS A NUMBER, y IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`the day` d m y MEANS Date d m y

-- "within n days after" an event: the period excludes the day of the event and ends on the nth day after it.
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER, start IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the nth day after` n start MEANS Date (Day start PLUS n)

-- "within 6 months after the date": the period ends on the same day number six months on; where that month is shorter,
-- on its last day (daydate's `add months` clamps, so 31 August gives 28 or 29 February).
GIVEN start IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`six months after` start MEANS `add months` start 6

GIVEN a IS A DATE, b IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE a `falls on or before` b IF Day a AT MOST Day b

-- The number of days from a to b (for the regulative rule, whose WITHIN counts days from the start of the period).
GIVEN a IS A DATE, b IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`days from` a `to` b MEANS Day b MINUS Day a
